<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ctesiphon</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ctesiphon was a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a> River in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, near present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>. It was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthian Empire</a> and then of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a>, whose great vaulted palace hall, the Taq Kasra, still partly stands. Captured by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> in the 630s, it declined after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a> founded Baghdad nearby.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ctesiphon.jpg" alt="Ctesiphon" width="296" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Renato de carvalho ferreira · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ctesiphon_map-en_reformat.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…755, eliminating the most dangerous rival power centre, and in 762 founded the Round City of Baghdad, Madinat al-Salam or &quot;City of Peace&quot;, on the western bank of the Tigris not far from the ruins of the Sasanian capital <b>Ctesiphon</b>, deliberately connecting Abbasid authority to Persian imperial prestige.</blockquote><a href="/empire/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 395 they launched their first large-scale attack on the Eastern Roman Empire, striking Thrace, overrunning Armenia, pillaging Cappadocia and entering Syria, while an invasion of the Sasanian Empire came close to <b>Ctesiphon</b> before a Persian counter-attack defeated it.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hunnic-empire">Hunnic Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…centres such as Seleucia on the Tigris, with its large Greek population, and the neighbouring royal residence of <b>Ctesiphon</b> served as commercial and administrative hubs, and the silver drachms struck by the Arsacid kings, inscribed in Greek, reflected the blend of Iranian and Hellenistic influences that shaped the…</blockquote><a href="/empire/parthian-empire">Parthian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…Sasanian general Rostam Farrokhzad after several days of fighting, opened Iraq to conquest and was followed by the fall of the Sasanian capital <b>Ctesiphon</b>, while the Battle of Nihawand in 642 CE, remembered in Islamic tradition as the “Victory of Victories”, broke the last organised Sasanian field army.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…(modern Fars in south-western Iran) and a vassal of the Arsacid Parthian dynasty, defeated and killed the last Parthian king, Artabanus IV, at the Battle of Hormozdgan, before taking the title Shahanshah, King of Kings, and entering the former Parthian capital of <b>Ctesiphon</b> on the Tigris around 226.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…in the twelfth century: Khaqani, whose prison poems and ode on the ruins of the Sasanian palace at <b>Ctesiphon</b> are classics of Persian verse, served the Shirvanshahs, and Nizami's Layla and Majnun, written for Akhsitan I, became one of the most influential romances of the Islamic world, retold…</blockquote><a href="/empire/shirvanshah-state">Shirvanshah State · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
